LESSONS_P3 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Material Request: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the Material Request and the demand machinery in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Material Request field walk; state the four request types and what each routes to; operate the three demand sources including reorder auto-generation; run the screening step that stands between demand and commitment; consolidate requests into purchase demand with the ordering arithmetic; handle transfer and issue requests at the module boundary; and run the demand reads.</p><p><b>What a Material Request is.</b> The Material Request is the demand document: a recorded statement that some part of the business needs some quantity of some item by some date. It commits nothing — no stock moves, no money is promised, no supplier is engaged. Its function is to make demand explicit, attributable, and reviewable before anything is committed, which is the structural difference between a purchasing function that responds to recorded demand and one that responds to corridor conversations.</p><p><b>Position in the flow.</b> Demand precedes commitment: the Material Request sits before the Request for Quotation and the Purchase Order (modules 4-5), and before the transfer machinery on the internal side. The sequence — need recorded, need screened, need consolidated, then committed — gives the buying decision its shape: <b>the Purchase Order commits against screened, consolidated demand, and every order line traces back to the requests that justified it.</b></p><p><b>The four types.</b> The Material Request Type field routes the demand:</p><p>1. <b>Purchase</b> — the demand becomes buying: the type this track's modules 4-5 consume.<br>2. <b>Material Transfer</b> — the demand is met from another warehouse: the branch replenishment lane the Inventory track's module 6 operates.<br>3. <b>Material Issue</b> — the demand is internal consumption: stock issued to a department or purpose through the issue lane.<br>4. <b>Manufacture</b> — the demand routes to production, where the site runs the manufacturing lane.</p><p>The type is a routing decision made at the request, and Chapter 6 covers the boundary cases — including the transfer request that becomes purchase demand when the network cannot cover it.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> Requests are raised by the roles the matrix permits (branch supervisors, department heads, and the reorder machinery itself); screening is the procurement function's step; approval bands per the matrix; and the standing rule: <b>demand is recorded before it is met — no order without its requests, no request without its stated need.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the field walk. Chapter 3: demand sources. Chapter 4: screening. Chapter 5: consolidation. Chapter 6: transfers and issues. Chapter 7: the demand reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Material Request doctype", "12", "<p>Open Material Request (awesome bar) &gt; New. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Material Request Type.</b> The routing decision (Chapter 1): Purchase, Material Transfer, Material Issue, or Manufacture. The type determines which machinery consumes the request and what the warehouse fields mean, so it is set first and set correctly — a transfer need typed as a purchase generates buying for stock the network already holds.</p><p><b>2. Requested By and the requesting context.</b> Who raises the request, and for which part of the business — the branch, department, or cost centre per the site's fields. Attribution matters operationally: the demand reads (Chapter 7) aggregate by requester, and the screening step (Chapter 4) evaluates need in the requester's context.</p><p><b>3. Required By Date.</b> The date the need becomes real — when the branch runs out, when the job starts, when the stock is needed on the shelf. The honest-date rule applies in a specific form here: the required-by date is the <b>need date, not the wish date</b>. Dates padded early teach the system's readers to discount them; dates entered late guarantee expediting. Every downstream schedule — the quotation cycle, the order's delivery date, the import consignment calendar — computes backward from this field.</p><p><b>4. The items table.</b> Per row:</p><p>1. <b>Item and quantity in the stated unit</b> — the unit column read per the standing discipline; branch requests in cartons and counts in units are the module 2 pairing rule's territory.<br>2. <b>The warehouse fields per type</b> — for Purchase: the warehouse the goods should arrive into; for Material Transfer: the source and target of the movement; for Material Issue: the warehouse issuing. The fields state where the need physically is.<br>3. <b>The per-row required-by date</b> where needs differ by line.</p><p><b>5. The justification.</b> The stated need — the reorder computation for auto-generated requests (Chapter 3), the branch's sales cover statement, the department's purpose for an issue. One line suffices; its presence is what the screening step reads, and a request whose need cannot be stated in a line is a request the screen returns to its raiser.</p><p><b>6. Save, submit, and the status life.</b> Submission enters the request into the demand pipeline. The status tracks its life: Pending, Partially Ordered, Ordered (for purchase types — the %ordered figure per row), Transferred or Issued for the internal types, and Stopped for demand deliberately ended (Chapter 7's hygiene). A request is never deleted to make it go away; it is stopped with its reason, so the demand record stays complete.</p><p><b>Verification per request.</b> The type correct for the need; the requester and context attributed; the date the need date; quantities in read units against stated warehouses; the justification present.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Demand sources", "12", "<p>Demand enters the pipeline from three sources. This chapter is each source and its rules.</p><p><b>1. Manual requests.</b> Branches and departments raise requests for what they need: the branch's stock request ahead of a promotion, the department's consumables, the clinic's equipment need. The rules:</p><p>1. <b>Raised by the permitted roles</b> in the requester's own context — the branch requests for the branch.<br>2. <b>Justified in a line</b> (Chapter 2.5) — the sales cover, the purpose, the event.<br>3. <b>Dated honestly</b> — the need date, computed from the requester's own arithmetic (the branch that sells forty units a week and holds eighty knows its date).</p><p><b>2. Reorder auto-generation.</b> The Inventory track's module 6 machinery: reorder levels on the item-warehouse rows, evaluated against <b>projected quantity</b> — actual stock minus reservations plus incoming orders — so the computation sees commitments in both directions. When projected quantity falls below the reorder level, the system generates a Material Request for the reorder quantity:</p><p>1. <b>The generated request carries its computation as its justification</b> — the level, the projected figure, the shortfall: screened like any other demand, not treated as pre-approved because a machine raised it.<br>2. <b>The parameters are module 2's planning fields</b> — measured lead times, costed safety stock, quantities in carton multiples: the auto-trigger is only as good as the fields it computes from, and the planning review is where its quality is maintained.<br>3. <b>The generation runs on the configured schedule</b>, and the reorder review (the weekly pass the Inventory track defined) reads what it generated before the screen consumes it.</p><p><b>3. Order-driven demand.</b> Sales commitments generate purchase demand where stock will not cover them: the enterprise order whose equipment must be bought (the Selling track's BizRetail Ltd case triggered exactly this), the made-to-order line, the special. The rules: the demand references its driving order, its date computes backward from the order's delivery schedule with the lead time (module 2's field), and its quantities are the order's quantities — demand that borrows an order's justification to buy extra is padding the screen exists to catch.</p><p><b>4. The source mix as information.</b> The demand pattern read (Chapter 7) splits the pipeline by source. The mix is operationally meaningful: a network whose demand is mostly auto-generated runs on its parameters (and its exceptions are parameter questions); one whose demand is mostly manual runs on judgment (and its exceptions are screening questions); order-driven spikes are the commercial calendar showing through. The read states the mix; the review acts on what it states.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The screening step", "12", "<p>Between recorded demand and committed money stands one procedure. This chapter is the screen.</p><p><b>1. What screening is.</b> The procurement function's review of submitted requests before they consolidate into buying: each request confirmed as a genuine, correctly-sized, correctly-dated need that the network cannot meet more cheaply from what it already holds. Screening runs on the demand pipeline's cadence (daily or per the volume), and its output is requests passed, returned, or resized — each with its note.</p><p><b>2. The screening checks, in order.</b></p><p>1. <b>Stock elsewhere first.</b> Before any purchase: does the network hold this item idle somewhere? The Inventory track's rebalancing lane (module 7) is the first answer to demand — the lateral transfer from a branch holding dead stock costs transport; the purchase costs the full landed figure. The screen queries network stock as its first act, and a purchase request for an item another warehouse holds in excess converts to a transfer request with the note.<br>2. <b>The need as stated.</b> The justification against the requester's context: the branch's sales cover arithmetic sampled against its actual sell-through (the reads the Inventory track's module 6 built serve here); the department's purpose against its pattern. Returned requests state what was missing.<br>3. <b>The size and the date.</b> Quantity against the stated need (the pairing rule catching unit errors — a branch requesting 15 when it means 15 cartons, or the reverse); the date against the real calendar.<br>4. <b>Budget and policy where configured</b> — the request against its cost centre's frame, per the site's budget configuration; policy items (restricted classes, approval-required categories) routed to their named approvers.</p><p><b>3. Approval bands.</b> Screened demand approves per the matrix: routine replenishment within the standing bands; above-band or exceptional demand to the named approvers with the screen's notes attached. The approval attaches to the demand before the money is committed — module 5's Purchase Order approval then reviews the commitment (supplier, price, terms), not the need, because the need was settled here. The two approvals ask different questions, and keeping them separate is what keeps both short.</p><p><b>4. The screen's record.</b> Each screening pass files its list: requests passed with sizes confirmed, returned with reasons, converted to transfers with the stock they found. The monthly screening read (Chapter 7) samples the passes — and the specific figure it tracks is stated plainly: <b>purchases avoided by the stock-elsewhere check</b>, in naira, because that figure is the screen's measurable contribution and the reason the step exists.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Consolidation", "12", "<p>Screened demand consolidates into buying. This chapter is the worksheet and the arithmetic.</p><p><b>1. Why consolidation.</b> The same item is demanded from several sources in the same window — the hub's reorder trigger, two branches' requests. Ordering each separately produces three small orders, three deliveries, three receipts, and none of the volume terms the supplier's price breaks offer. Consolidation combines the demand into one purchase decision per item per supplier per window, and the trace (below) keeps every source visible inside it.</p><p><b>2. The consolidation worksheet.</b> Per buying window, per item:</p><p>1. <b>The demand lines</b> — each open, screened request row for the item: source, quantity, required-by date, destination warehouse.<br>2. <b>The consolidated quantity</b> — the sum, adjusted by the ordering arithmetic below.<br>3. <b>The date structure</b> — the earliest genuine need date governs the order's first delivery; where sources need at different dates, the consolidated order carries the schedule (module 5's per-row dates and split deliveries serve exactly this).<br>4. <b>The destination structure</b> — where the goods should land: the hub for onward distribution in the standing network pattern, or split destinations where the supplier delivers direct per the arrangement.</p><p><b>3. The ordering arithmetic.</b> The consolidated quantity is shaped by module 2's constraints, applied in order: the purchase unit's multiples (orders in whole cartons), the supplier's minimums, the price-break thresholds where crossing one is priced against carrying the extra (the module 4 quotations state the breaks; the worksheet shows the comparison), and the import consignment arithmetic on foreign lines. Each adjustment above the raw demand is written on the worksheet with its reason — the excess is a decided figure, not a rounding accident.</p><p><b>4. The request-to-order trace.</b> The Purchase Order (module 5) creates against the consolidated demand, and the system links each order row to its requests: %ordered advances on every source request as the order commits. The trace answers, in both directions, the two standing questions: which requests does this order serve (the order's justification), and where is my request (the requester's status view). A consolidation that loses its trace turns the demand pipeline into a suggestion box; the linkage is maintained as part of creating the order, not reconstructed afterward.</p><p><b>5. The window discipline.</b> Consolidation runs on the buying calendar — the weekly window for domestic replenishment, the consignment calendar for imports — and demand arriving after a window closes waits for the next unless its date cannot wait, in which case it is the expedite lane with its named approval and its premium visible. The calendar is what makes consolidation possible; a pipeline ordered continuously in dribbles consolidates nothing.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Transfer and issue requests", "12", "<p>Two request types stay internal. This chapter is their handling and the boundary where internal demand becomes buying.</p><p><b>1. Material Transfer requests.</b> The branch replenishment lane: the branch (or the reorder machinery evaluating the branch's rows) requests stock from the hub. The mechanics belong to the Inventory track's module 6 — the weekly replenishment run, the two-step transfer with the transit warehouse, the receiving confirmation — and this module's contribution is the demand side: the request raised, dated, and justified per Chapter 2, consumed by the transfer run.</p><p><b>2. The cascade: when transfer demand becomes purchase demand.</b> The standing network pattern is hub-and-spoke: branches request from the hub; the hub buys from suppliers. The cascade rule:</p><p>1. <b>The branch's request is always a transfer request</b> — the branch's relationship is with the hub, and branch-level purchasing is the exception lane the network policy defines (the Inventory track's exception purchase lane), not a routing choice.<br>2. <b>The hub's coverage check</b> — the transfer run evaluates the hub's projected stock against the requested transfers: covered demand transfers on the run's schedule.<br>3. <b>Uncovered demand cascades</b> — the shortfall becomes purchase demand at the hub: the hub's reorder row may already have triggered (the projected figure fell as the transfer reservations landed), or the screen raises the purchase demand explicitly with the branch requests as its justification. Either way the consolidation worksheet (Chapter 5) shows the branch demand inside the purchase quantity, and the trace runs branch request → hub purchase → order.</p><p><b>3. Material Issue requests.</b> Internal consumption — consumables to a department, materials to a project — requested, screened for purpose, and issued through the Inventory issue lane with the cost landing on the consuming context per the accountant's design. The issue request's discipline is the purpose statement: issues aggregate into the consumption reads, and unattributed consumption is the leak those reads exist to find.</p><p><b>4. The boundary, stated.</b> This module owns demand and its screening; the Inventory track owns the movement machinery (transfers, issues, the transit discipline); modules 4-5 own the buying that uncovered demand becomes. The documents keep the boundary visible: the request states the need, the transfer or issue documents the internal movement, the order documents the commitment — one need, each stage on its own record, the trace connecting them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The demand reads", "12", "<p>The standing reads on the demand pipeline, each with its cadence and action.</p><p><b>1. The open-demand read — weekly.</b> All open requests by age, status, and required-by date:</p><p>1. <b>Approaching dates</b> — open requests whose need dates fall inside the buying lead time: each is either in a consolidation window, on an order (%ordered advancing), or a problem named now while expediting is still an option.<br>2. <b>The aging tail</b> — requests open past the policy's window with no ordering activity: each chased to its state — superseded (stopped with reason), waiting on a window (noted), or lost in the pipeline (the process finding).</p><p><b>2. The %ordered read — weekly, with the open-demand pass.</b> Partially ordered requests: the unordered remainder confirmed as still needed (demand shrinks as reality changes — the remainder is re-screened, not automatically carried) or stopped. The figure per request is the requester's answer to <i>where is my request</i>, and keeping it true is what makes the pipeline trustworthy to its users.</p><p><b>3. The stale and stopped hygiene — monthly.</b> Requests stopped in the month with their reasons aggregated; open requests with passed dates and no action swept into decisions. The pipeline's target state matches every other register in the programme: only live demand, because the consolidation worksheets and the buying calendar compute from it.</p><p><b>4. The demand pattern read — monthly.</b> The pipeline by source (Chapter 3.4), requester, and item class:</p><p>1. <b>The source mix</b> and its movement — auto-generation's share rising as parameters mature is the intended direction; manual spikes trace to their events.<br>2. <b>The requester patterns</b> — branches whose requests consistently resize at screening (over-asking as negotiation) are a conversation with data; branches that never request until stock-out are the opposite conversation.<br>3. <b>The screening figures</b> — returned and converted requests by reason, and the purchases-avoided figure (Chapter 4.4) stated in naira.</p><p><b>5. The trace audit — quarterly.</b> A sample of the quarter's Purchase Orders walked backward: each order row to its requests, each request to its justification. The audit confirms the standing rule holds — no order without its demand — and its findings are process corrections: the order raised without requests (the corridor purchase the pipeline exists to end), the trace lost at consolidation, the justification that was a formality. The audit files with the procurement function's quarterly records, alongside the module 8 scorecard cycle.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the 300-carton consolidation", "13", "<p>This chapter runs the demand machinery through the standing case: the PARA-500 purchase that module 5 will place with Emzor. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the auto-trigger (Chapter 3).</b> The weekly reorder evaluation at Central Warehouse computes PARA-500's projected quantity — actual stock minus the sales reservations, plus nothing incoming — below the reorder level. The machinery generates a Material Request, type Purchase, for <b>260 cartons</b>, carrying its computation as the justification: the level, the projected figure, the shortfall, the reorder quantity in carton multiples per the planning fields.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the branch requests (Chapters 3, 6).</b> The same week, two branches raise transfer requests for PARA-500 ahead of their local demand: <b>Ibadan for 15 cartons</b>, <b>Akure for 25 cartons</b> — each justified with its sales cover arithmetic, each dated to its need. The transfer run's coverage check finds the hub's projected stock — already below reorder — cannot cover the transfers: the branch demand cascades into the hub's purchase demand per the cascade rule, the branch requests standing as part of its justification.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the screen (Chapter 4).</b> The screening pass works the three demands: the stock-elsewhere check runs first and finds no idle PARA-500 anywhere in the network (the rebalancing lane has nothing to offer — this is genuine new demand); the branch arithmetic samples clean against sell-through; the units read correctly (cartons throughout, per the pairing rule); the dates hold. All three pass, with the screen's notes filed.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the consolidation worksheet (Chapter 5).</b> The buying window consolidates PARA-500: 260 (Central reorder) + 15 (Ibadan) + 25 (Akure) = <b>300 cartons</b>. The ordering arithmetic confirms the figure: whole cartons already; above Emzor's minimum; the quantity sits at the volume band the standing arrangement prices (module 4's cycle established the rates — the order will place at <b>₦9,600 per carton</b>). The date structure records the earliest genuine need — Central's cover date — and notes that the branches' dates allow the second drop: the worksheet anticipates the <b>two-drop schedule</b> module 5 will commit (180 next week, 120 the week after), driven by the dates the requests stated.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the trace stands (Chapters 5, 7).</b> Module 5's Purchase Order will create against this consolidated demand, and at that moment %ordered advances on all three requests: the Central trigger, Ibadan's 15, Akure's 25 — each requester's status view answering where their request went. The quarter's trace audit will walk this order backward and find the complete chain: order → worksheet → three requests → three justifications — the standing rule holding: no order without its demand.</p><p><b>The pipeline evidence.</b> The requests with their justifications; the screen's pass with the stock-elsewhere check recorded; the worksheet with the arithmetic and the date structure; the trace ready for the order. Module 4 covers how the Emzor rates on this worksheet were established; module 5 places the commitment.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in demand management, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the corridor purchase.</b> Symptom: Purchase Orders raised from conversations with no requests behind them; demand invisible, unattributable, unscreened. Correction: the demand recorded retroactively where the purchase was genuine; the trace audit's finding filed. Prevention: the standing rule enforced in configuration where the site gates orders on requests, and the quarterly trace audit.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the wish date.</b> Symptom: required-by dates padded early as negotiation or entered late as afterthought; downstream schedules computing from fiction; readers discounting all dates. Correction: dates corrected to need dates at screening. Prevention: the need-date rule and the screening date check.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the purchase for stock the network holds.</b> Symptom: buying while another warehouse holds the item in excess; dead stock accumulating at one site while money is spent at another. Correction: the request converted to a transfer; the purchase avoided. Prevention: the stock-elsewhere check as the screen's first act, with its avoided-purchases figure read monthly.</p><p><b>Error 4 — auto-generation treated as pre-approved.</b> Symptom: machine-generated requests ordered without screening; parameter errors (a stale reorder level, an optimistic lead time) buying at scale. Correction: the parameters reviewed; the excess handled through the stock-health lanes. Prevention: generated requests screened like any other, and the planning review maintaining the fields.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the lost trace.</b> Symptom: orders that cannot name their requests; requesters who cannot find their demand; %ordered figures meaning nothing. Correction: the linkage rebuilt for open documents. Prevention: the trace maintained as part of order creation, and the quarterly audit.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the immortal request.</b> Symptom: open requests aging past their dates and their needs; the pipeline cluttered with dead demand; consolidation worksheets summing fiction. Correction: the aging tail swept — stopped with reasons or re-dated as re-screened demand. Prevention: the weekly open-demand read and the monthly hygiene.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the request walk with the type routing, need dates, and justifications; operate the three demand sources including the auto-trigger's computation; run the four screening checks in order with the approval-band separation; consolidate with the worksheet, the ordering arithmetic, and the window discipline; handle the transfer cascade and the issue lane at the boundary; and run the five demand reads.</p><p><b>Module rule 3: demand is recorded before it is met — typed correctly, dated to the need, justified in a line, screened with stock-elsewhere first, consolidated by the worksheet's written arithmetic, and traced so that every order names its requests and every requester can find their demand.</b> Module 4 covers Requests for Quotation and the comparison cycle.</p>"),
]

